#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049459 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048877 :topic/math-academy-id 1932 :topic/title "Partial Differentiability of Multivariable Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048917 :topic/math-academy-id 3344 :topic/title "Visualizing Vector Fields"}] :topic/id #uuid "f6fd4776-56f0-5112-9cdc-83e344004d93" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133876 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133692 :question/id #uuid "78e7cbb2-d207-5d5e-bf46-5c3b2686933f" :question/math-academy-id "e-8740" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=\\ln(2x+y)\\,\\mathbf{i}+ x^2e^{3y}\\,\\mathbf{j}.$ \nEvaluate $\\nabla \\cdot\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{F}=\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F=\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{e}^{3y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2x+y}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{3y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf {F}$ at the point $(1,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(1,0) & =\\frac{2}{2(1)+0}+3(1)^{2}{e}^{3(0)} \\\\  & =1+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Two Dimensions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133880 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133696 :question/id #uuid "ac6e7a2c-19a3-595a-ba46-f38c653a9bec" :question/math-academy-id "e-8927" :question/problem "![](images/d3/d37f2a445986b2aae60603fed3bef1b480d8f049dfe17a0cf26325ac2eb167d8.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf F?$\n\nI. $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = 0$\n\nII. $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F > 0$\n\nIII. $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F <                    0$\n\nIV. $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\textrm{div}\\, \\mathbf F = \\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $\\mathbf F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $\\mathbf F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $\\nabla \\cdot  \\mathbf F > 0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $\\nabla \\cdot  \\mathbf F <         0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $\\nabla \\cdot  \\mathbf F =0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = 0$ everywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf F,$ we say that $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $\\mathbf F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = 0$ everywhere, the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Divergence"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133889 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133705 :question/id #uuid "3cbc5b4a-dde4-59b0-bc34-08c4d8776e0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9489" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=(2x-y)^2\\,\\mathbf{i}+(3y-2z)^2\\,\\mathbf{j}+(x-2z)^2\\,\\mathbf{k}.$ Evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j} + R\\,\\mathbf k$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{F}=\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}+\\dfrac{\\partial R}{\\partial z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((2x-y)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((3y-2z)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}((x-2z)^{2}) \\\\  & =4(2x-y)+6(3y-2z)-4(x-2z) \\\\  & =4x+14y-4z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf { F}$ at the point $(1,2,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F(1,2,1) & =4(1)+14(2)-4(1) \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Three Dimensions"}] :topic/math-academy-id 2131 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049765 :topic/math-academy-id 3701 :topic/title "Properties of the Divergence Operator"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050694 :topic/math-academy-id 3361 :topic/title "Green's Theorem in Flux Form"}] :topic/title "The Divergence of a Vector Field"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049481 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048593 :topic/math-academy-id 35 :topic/title "The Power Rule for Differentiation"}] :topic/id #uuid "f8a6d16e-1a41-5d40-8c3d-467ebdcc3782" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064167 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064038 :question/id #uuid "368cbbae-4eff-5eff-a8f3-d3e8359447a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-364" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int x^2 \\,\\textrm d x$." :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=2$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int x^2 \\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac{x^{2+1}}{2+1} + C = \\dfrac{x^3}{3}+C\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{x^{3}}{3} + C) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{x^{3}}{3}) + \\frac{d}{dx}(C) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{d}{dx}(x^{3}) + 0 &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 3x^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= x^{2}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Monomial"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064168 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064039 :question/id #uuid "818f4fbe-e251-57bd-90ae-b6517c6f2f89" :question/math-academy-id "e-367" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt x$." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the square root as a rational power,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt x= x^{1/2}\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the power rule with\n\n$$\nn=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nand get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\sqrt{x}dx &= \\intx^{1/2}dx \\\\\n&= (x^{1/2 + 1})/((\\frac{1}{2} + 1)) + C \\\\\n&= (x^{3/2})/((\\frac{3}{2})) + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{2x^{3/2}}{3} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{2x^{3/2}}{3} + C) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{2x^{3/2}}{3}) + \\frac{d}{dx}(C) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2}{3}\\frac{d}{dx}(x^{3/2}) + 0 &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2}{3} \\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x^{3/2 - 1} &=  \\\\\n&= x^{1/2}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Radical Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064169 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064040 :question/id #uuid "c25e6037-3900-5314-b9a9-262fef07061c" :question/math-academy-id "e-368" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\dfrac 1 {x^3}$ and that $F'(x) = f(x)$, calculate $F(x)$." :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if\n\n$$\nF'(x) = f(x)\n$$\n\nthen integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF^{′}(x) &= f(x) \\\\\n\\intF^{′}(x)dx &= \\intf(x)dx \\\\\nF(x) &= \\intf(x)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {x^3}= x^{-3}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the power rule with $n=-3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(x) &= \\intf(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int\\frac{1}{x^{3}}dx \\\\\n&= \\intx^{-3}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{x^{-3 + 1}}{-3 + 1} + C \\\\\n&=-\\frac{x^{-2}}{2} + C \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2x^{2}} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Reciprocal Function"}] :topic/math-academy-id 308 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048442 :topic/math-academy-id 43 :topic/title "The Constant Multiple Rule for Indefinite Integrals"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050057 :topic/math-academy-id 283 :topic/title "The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus"}] :topic/title "The Antiderivative"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049486 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049592 :topic/math-academy-id 3215 :topic/title "Introduction to Differential Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271 :topic/math-academy-id 1007 :topic/title "The Chain Rule With Exponential Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050789 :topic/math-academy-id 305 :topic/title "The Chain Rule With Trigonometric Functions"}] :topic/id #uuid "f8bc5d87-e505-5270-9efd-ad77aaf46e52" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100888 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100725 :question/id #uuid "e092e191-84ac-5d9f-b0db-94d628a6154e" :question/math-academy-id "e-525" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny' = -\\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad x \\neq 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f'(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$\n\n- Substituting $y = f(x)$ and $y' = f'(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ and $y' = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100951 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100795 :question/id #uuid "f5ffa549-e77e-5db4-9fde-5b0134957539" :question/math-academy-id "e-8732" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{3y}{x}, \\qquad y(-1) = -2.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-1) = -2.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}{-1}}) = {\\color{blue}{-2}}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{x}^{3} \\\\ -2 & =A(-1)^{3} \\\\ -2 & =A(-1) \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = 2x^3 .\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Initial Value Problems"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100959 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100801 :question/id #uuid "1228900d-87d4-5ff0-8e01-ff9417e82220" :question/math-academy-id "e-9978" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y = e^x + 1$\n\n- $y = -e^x$\n\n- $y = 2e^x$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y = e^x + 1,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ {e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}{e}^{x}+1 \\\\ 0 & \\ne 1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y = e^x+1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = -e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ -{e}^{x} & =-{e}^{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = -e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = 2e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ 2{e}^{x} & =2{e}^{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = 2e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1181 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048451 :topic/math-academy-id 2086 :topic/title "Introduction to Systems of Linear ODEs"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049380 :topic/math-academy-id 1061 :topic/title "Solving First-Order ODEs Using Direct Integration"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049383 :topic/math-academy-id 2976 :topic/title "Qualitative Analysis of First-Order ODEs"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050032 :topic/math-academy-id 2549 :topic/title "The Superposition Principle"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050150 :topic/math-academy-id 2547 :topic/title "Linear Independence of Solutions to Homogeneous ODEs"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050206 :topic/math-academy-id 2543 :topic/title "Power Series Solutions of Differential Equations"}] :topic/title "Verifying Solutions of Differential Equations"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049488 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048867 :topic/math-academy-id 203 :topic/title "Simplifying Expressions Using Basic Trigonometric Identities"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050714 :topic/math-academy-id 1453 :topic/title "Using the Pythagorean Identity in the First Quadrant"}] :topic/id #uuid "f8e1bb8b-b5d4-5d15-8a60-0a6ff136ccb6" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059641 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059469 :question/id #uuid "762e323d-62be-5cd0-ad06-7b11af56fe3f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3136" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\sin^4{\\theta}+\\sin^2{\\theta}\\cos^2{\\theta}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor $\\sin^2\\theta$ from the expression:\n\n$$\n\\sin^4{\\theta}+\\sin^2{\\theta}\\cos^2{\\theta} = \\sin^2{\\theta}\\cdot(\\sin^2{\\theta}+\\cos^2{\\theta})\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta = 1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot ({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}) & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin^4{\\theta}+\\sin^2{\\theta}\\cos^2{\\theta} = \\sin^2\\theta.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression by Factoring and Using the Pythagorean Identity"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059642 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059470 :question/id #uuid "f2b7e482-2bcd-5bd2-91ee-66415b375da4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3137" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^3 \\theta}{\\sin\\theta}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^3 \\theta} {\\sin \\theta}\t=\t\\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\cdot (\\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^2 \\theta)} {\\sin \\theta}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot ({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (1)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^3 \\theta} {\\sin \\theta} = \\cot \\theta.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Rational Expression Using the Pythagorean Identity"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059643 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059471 :question/id #uuid "9ee1e04e-8a36-5597-8928-c2ebd67c1b1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3139" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\dfrac{\\sin{\\theta}}{1+\\cos{\\theta}}+\\cot{\\theta}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the second term using the identity $\\cot \\theta = \\dfrac{\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sin{\\theta}}{1+\\cos{\\theta}}+\\dfrac{\\cos{\\theta}}{\\sin{\\theta}}\n$$\n\nNext, we put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} & = \\\\ \\frac{({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} & = \\\\ \\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & = \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sin{\\theta}}{1+\\cos{\\theta}}+\\cot{\\theta} = \\csc\\theta.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Trigonometric Expression Using a Common Denominator and the Pythagorean Identity"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059655 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059486 :question/id #uuid "d917e025-59a4-5df5-b412-4eb6f2c4afc7" :question/math-academy-id "e-3329" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\sin x(2\\cos x - \\sin x) - \\cos^2x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 x$ and $\\cos^2 x$ terms. \n\n$$\n2\\sin x\\cos x -(\\sin^2x + \\cos^2 x)\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2{x} + \\cos^2{x} = 1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n2\\sin x\\cos x - 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin x(2\\cos x - \\sin x) - \\cos^2x = 2\\sin x\\cos x - 1.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression by Expanding Parentheses and Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :topic/math-academy-id 207 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048314 :topic/math-academy-id 292 :topic/title "Special Limits Involving Cosine"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048418 :topic/math-academy-id 1837 :topic/title "The Arc Length of a Vector-Valued Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048430 :topic/math-academy-id 1076 :topic/title "Writing Sums of Trigonometric Functions in Amplitude-Phase Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048612 :topic/math-academy-id 264 :topic/title "Simplifying Trigonometric Expressions Using Cofunction Identities"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050932 :topic/math-academy-id 205 :topic/title "Alternate Forms of the Pythagorean Identity"}] :topic/title "Simplifying Expressions Using the Pythagorean Identity"}]}
